\label{lem2}
The eigenvalues $\rho_1$, $\rho_2$ of $\mathcal{A}_\omega$ are simple for $\lambda\in (0,1]$, and we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rho}
|\rho_j-1|\le  \frac{2+\sqrt{7}}{2}\lambda \quad (j=1,2).
\end{equation}

There exist $M_\omega\in \mathcal{M}_2(\mathbb{C})$ invertible such that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:A_omega}
\mathcal{A}_\omega = M_\omega
\begin{pmatrix}
\rho_1 & 0\\
0 & \rho_2
\end{pmatrix}
M_\omega^{-1}
\end{equation}
with $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$ the two eigenvalues of the matrix $\mathcal{A}_\omega$ and 
$$
  \|(\mathcal{A}_\omega)^n\|_2 \le \kappa_2(M_\omega),                                    
$$
where $ \kappa_2(M_\omega):=\|M_\omega\|_2\|M^{-1}_\omega\|_2$ is the condition number, which is here bounded with respect to $\omega\in \mathbb{R}$ and $\lambda\in [0,1]$.
